          <SocialProof users="9.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#causes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Five Causes</a>
          <a href="#water" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Watering Problems</a>
          <a href="#temperature" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Cold and Humidity</a>
          <a href="#product" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Product Problems</a>
          <a href="#fixing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How to Fix It</a>
          <a href="#partial" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. When Only Some Joints Fail</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Polymeric sand hardens through a one-time chemical reaction between water and the polymer binder. That reaction has a window, and once it has passed — either because it completed badly or because the binder is spent — no amount of additional water restarts it.
        </p>

        <div id="causes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Five Reasons Polymeric Sand Stays Soft
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tell-tale Sign</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Can You Rescue It?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Too little water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard crust on top, loose sand beneath</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sometimes, if within hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Too much water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Binder visible on paver faces, joints under-filled</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, binder has left the joint</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cold weather</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sand stayed soft everywhere, cool conditions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sometimes, with warmth and time</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Damp joints at install</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard clumps in otherwise loose sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, reaction happened early</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Old or spoiled product</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No hardening at all despite correct method</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, binder is spent</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The One Case Where More Water Helps</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Under-watering, caught within a few hours.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you watered too lightly and realise the same day, a careful second saturation can still reach and activate the dry binder underneath. Beyond roughly a day, the surface has cured into a crust that sheds water rather than letting it through, and further watering just runs off. Every other cause on this list is not fixable with a hose.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Cause One and Two: Water, Too Little or Too Much
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Too Little Water</h3>

          <p>
            The most common cause by a wide margin. Polymeric sand needs saturating through the full depth of the joint, and a quick pass with a hose wets perhaps the top quarter inch. The polymer there activates and forms a crust; everything beneath stays dry, loose and unbound. Because the surface looks and feels convincingly hard, people do not discover the problem until the crust breaks underfoot or in a storm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturers specify watering in several light passes precisely to avoid this — each pass soaks in and carries water deeper rather than running off. The test is not whether the surface is wet but whether water is reaching the bottom of the joint, which in practice means watering until you see water standing briefly in the joint before it drains.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Too Much Water, or Too Fast</h3>

          <p>
            Overwatering causes a different failure that presents similarly. A heavy flow or a jet nozzle carries the binder out of the joint before it can react with the sand, depositing it on the paver faces where it cures as haze. What is left in the joint is sand with insufficient binder, which never develops strength no matter how long you wait.
          </p>

          <p>
            This one is not recoverable by watering, because the binder is physically no longer in the joint. The sand has to come out and be replaced. The presence of haze on the paver surfaces alongside soft joints is the signature of this failure, and it distinguishes it from simple under-watering.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Cause Three: Temperature and Humidity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand cures chemically, and like most chemical reactions it slows dramatically as temperature falls. Most manufacturers specify a minimum of roughly 50°F, and that applies to the surface and the joints as well as the air — a patio in shade on a cool day can be well below the air temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            Below that threshold the reaction does not stop permanently so much as stall. Sand installed in marginal conditions sometimes hardens weeks later when genuinely warm weather arrives, which is why patience is worth trying before excavating joints in autumn. It is equally common for it never to develop full strength, and to remain crumbly through the following season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Humidity Slows the Cure</h3>

          <p>
            High humidity extends curing times considerably, because the water in the joint has less capacity to evaporate and the material stays saturated longer. Sand that would be firm in 24 hours on a dry breezy day can take two or three times as long in muggy conditions. Soft joints 48 hours after installation in humid weather are not necessarily a failure — give it several more days of dry weather before concluding anything.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Check that both air and surface temperatures were above about 50°F during and after installation.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Overnight lows matter as much as daytime highs; a cold night stalls a cure that started well.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Shaded sections of a patio cure more slowly than sunlit ones and may lag by days.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>In humid weather, allow substantially longer before judging the result.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>If autumn is closing in, waiting for spring warmth is a legitimate strategy before deciding to redo it.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="product" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cause Four and Five: Damp Joints and Old Product
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Installed Into Damp Joints</h3>

          <p>
            Moisture in the joints when the sand goes in begins activating the polymer immediately, while the sand is still being swept and levelled. The binder is consumed forming small hard clumps in isolation rather than a continuous bound mass. The signature is distinctive: pockets of genuinely hard material scattered through sand that is otherwise loose. Nothing recovers this — the binder has already reacted.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Old, Damp or Spoiled Product</h3>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand has a shelf life, and its enemy is exactly what activates it. A bag that has been stored in a damp garage, has sat on a concrete floor absorbing moisture, or has been opened and resealed for a season may have partially reacted inside the bag. It will pour and sweep normally and simply never harden.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the bag before blaming your technique. Hard lumps inside a sealed bag are a clear sign the product has taken on moisture. Store polymeric sand off the floor, sealed, and under cover, and buy fresh for each project rather than carrying a part-used bag through a winter.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">A Practical Precaution</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Test a small area before doing the whole patio.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Install and water a couple of square feet, wait 24 hours in decent weather, and press a screwdriver into it. Ten minutes of work and one day of patience confirms both your product and your technique before you commit an entire patio and several bags to the same method.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="fixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Fix Joints That Never Hardened
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you have established the cause, the path forward is fairly clear-cut.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">If you under-watered today, water again properly</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The only genuinely recoverable case. Saturate in several light passes until water reaches the joint bottom. Beyond about a day the surface crust sheds water and this stops working.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">If the weather was cold or humid, wait</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Give it several more days of warm dry weather, or in late autumn wait for spring. Stalled cures sometimes complete; excavating in October is premature.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Otherwise, remove the failed sand</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A shop vacuum, joint scraper or stiff brush. Loose sand comes out readily. Any hard clumps need breaking up and lifting out with it.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Verify your product before reinstalling</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Open a fresh bag and check for lumps. If the old bag had hardened material inside, it was the problem and reusing it repeats the failure.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Confirm the joints are bone dry</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A full dry day after any washing or rain. This is the step that caused the failure in the damp-joint case.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Reinstall in stages with consolidation</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sweep in, tamp or vibrate, top up, repeat until joints sit about an eighth of an inch below the paver surface.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clear the paver faces completely</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Broom and then a leaf blower at a shallow angle before any water touches it.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">8</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Water correctly and watch the runoff</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Shower setting, several passes, saturating the full depth. Milky runoff means binder is leaving the joints.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Do not sweep fresh polymeric sand on top of sand that failed to harden. The new material cures as a shallow cap over loose sand, has no depth of grip on the paver faces, and breaks up within a season. The old material genuinely has to come out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="partial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When Only Some of the Joints Stayed Soft
          </h2>

          <p>
            Patchy results are common and genuinely informative, because the pattern of which joints hardened tells you what went wrong.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pattern</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Points To</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Response</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shaded areas soft, sunlit areas firm</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Temperature or slower drying in shade</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wait; often completes with warm dry weather</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wide joints soft, narrow joints firm</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Product not rated for the wider joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Redo wide joints with a wide-joint product</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Deep joints soft at the bottom only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water never reached full depth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove and reinstall with proper watering</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Random patches soft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Uneven watering coverage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove the soft areas and redo</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One end of the patio soft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ran out of time, product or daylight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Redo that section</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hard clumps in loose sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Damp joints at installation</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove entirely; binder already spent</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Repairing Sections Rather Than the Whole Patio</h3>

          <p>
            Sectional repair is legitimate when the sound joints genuinely are sound. Test them properly first — press a screwdriver into several joints across the good area rather than judging by appearance, because a surface crust over loose sand feels convincing underfoot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be aware of two practical consequences. Freshly installed polymeric sand often differs slightly in colour from sand that has weathered for a season, and that difference can persist. And the boundary between old and new joints is a discontinuity where water tends to find a path, so it is worth extending the repair a joint or two beyond the visibly failed area rather than stopping exactly at the edge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Watering Repeatedly Days Later</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Once the top of a joint has cured into a crust, further watering runs off rather than penetrating to the dry binder underneath. Re-watering only helps in the narrow case of under-watering caught within hours. In every other case it wets the paver faces, risks moving surface residue around, and changes nothing in the joint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming Technique Without Checking the Bag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polymeric sand partially reacts if it takes on moisture in storage, and a bag kept in a damp garage or opened and carried over a winter may simply have no active binder left. Hard lumps inside a sealed bag confirm it. Check the product before excavating a patio and repeating the same installation with the same sand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the Result Too Early in Humid Weather</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High humidity can easily double or triple curing time, and shaded sections lag behind sunlit ones by days. Soft joints 48 hours after installation in muggy conditions are not proof of failure. Allow several more days of genuinely dry weather before concluding that anything has gone wrong.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sweeping New Sand Over Unhardened Sand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The fresh material cures as a thin cap over loose sand beneath, with no depth of grip on the paver faces, and it breaks up within a season. Unhardened sand has to be removed completely — vacuumed or scraped out — before new sand goes in, however tempting the shortcut looks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing Late in the Season and Hoping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most polymeric sands need surface and air temperatures above roughly 50°F, including overnight, for the polymer to cure. Installing in a cold snap and hoping tends to produce sand that never develops full strength. If the season has turned, waiting for spring gives a far better result than a marginal autumn install.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my polymeric sand not hardening?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually too little water, so the polymer activated only at the surface and left dry sand beneath a crust. The other causes are overwatering that carried binder out of the joints, curing below about 50°F, installing into damp joints, or a bag that absorbed moisture in storage and has no active binder left.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I add more water to polymeric sand that did not harden?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you under-watered and realise within a few hours. After roughly a day the surface has cured into a crust that sheds water rather than letting it reach the dry binder underneath, so further watering runs off. In every other cause, more water does not help and the sand needs replacing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does polymeric sand take to harden?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It typically firms within 24 hours and reaches working strength in 24 to 48, but humidity, cool temperatures and shade all extend that considerably. In muggy weather it can take two or three times as long, so soft joints two days after installation in humid conditions are not necessarily a failure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does cold weather stop polymeric sand from curing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Most products need surface and air temperatures above roughly 50°F, including overnight. Below that the reaction stalls rather than stopping permanently — sand installed in marginal conditions sometimes hardens when genuinely warm weather arrives, which is why waiting is worth trying before excavating in autumn.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can polymeric sand go bad in the bag?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Moisture is what activates it, so a bag stored in a damp garage, sitting on a concrete floor, or opened and carried through a season can partially react inside the bag. It will pour and sweep normally and never harden. Hard lumps inside a sealed bag confirm it has spoiled.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to remove polymeric sand that did not harden?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most cases yes. Sweeping fresh sand over unhardened sand produces a thin cured cap over loose material with no grip on the paver faces, and it breaks up within a season. The exceptions are under-watering caught the same day, and a stalled cure in cold weather that may still complete.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

