        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Polymeric sand hardens once, through a chemical reaction with water, and that reaction has a window. Under-watering leaves dry binder beneath a convincing crust. Overwatering carries the binder onto the pavers. Cold stalls the reaction, damp joints start it early, and a bag that took on moisture in storage has already spent it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Which cause you have determines everything, so diagnose before acting. More water genuinely helps in one scenario only — under-watering caught within a few hours, before the surface crust forms and starts shedding water. In cold or humid weather, patience is the right move and excavating in October is premature.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For every other cause, the sand has to come out. Vacuum or scrape the joints empty, check the bag for lumps before trusting the same product again, let everything dry completely, and reinstall in stages with proper watering. Sweeping new sand over sand that failed is the one approach that reliably fails twice.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And before you commit a whole patio to any method, test two square feet of it. Install, water, wait a day in decent weather, then press a screwdriver into the joint. Ten minutes of work and one day of patience validates both your product and your technique, which is a great deal cheaper than discovering a spoiled bag after eight bags have gone down.
          </p>
        </div>

